@page "/forms/expense"
@* Resuming a saved draft. The id is in the path rather than the query so the browser's back button and
   a bookmark both land on the same form - and it is checked against the caller's session at the
   server, so pasting somebody else's id gets "could not be found" rather than their claim. *@
@page "/forms/expense/{DraftId:guid}"
@implements IDisposable
@using Grpc.Core
@using GSBC.Accounting.Shared.Contracts.Entities.Features.Expenses
@using GSBC.Accounting.Shared.Contracts.Messages.Requests.Features.Expenses
@using GSBC.Accounting.Shared.Contracts.Messages.Responses.Base
@using GSBC.Accounting.Shared.Contracts.Messages.Responses.Features.Expenses
@using GSBC.Accounting.Shared.Contracts.Services.Features.Expenses
@using GSBC.Accounting.WASM.Features.Expenses
@using GSBC.Accounting.WASM.Features.Expenses.Components
@inject IExpenseSubmissionService Submissions
@* Only the mock-data button uploads from this page - the real uploads belong to ExpenseDetailsSection,
   which owns the dropzones. Injected unconditionally rather than behind a directive: Razor has no
   conditional @inject, and an unused scoped client costs nothing. *@
@inject AttachmentClient Attachments
@* Only for the current path, which the refusal banner's links have to be written against - see
   PagePath, and SectionRail.Href for the trap they are both avoiding. *@
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@* Section 1's answers from the last claim filed on this browser. Read on a NEW form only - a resumed
   draft already carries its own. *@
@inject ClaimantMemory Claimant

<PageTitle>@(Model.Kind is null ? UnchosenTitle : Text.DocumentTitle)</PageTitle>

@*
    BOTH expense forms, on one page. Section 0 asks which one, and everything below it is that form's
    own words: the two documents share all eight sections structurally and almost none of their wording,
    so the structure is written once here and every string comes from ExpenseFormWording, keyed by kind.

    Sections 1 to 6 are fillable; 7 and 8 are rendered read-only and disabled, because an approver and
    finance complete them after submission and neither is on this page.

    What this page must never do is share a string between the kinds to save an @if. Two of the six
    compliance questions and one of the five declarations are word-for-word identical; the rest are not,
    and a form that asks a subtly different question has recorded an answer to a question nobody asked.
    See docs/modules/expenses/paper-form-fields.md, which was read out of the .docx files.
*@

<div class="layout">
    <SectionRail Sections="Rail"/>

    <main>
        @if (Model.Kind is not null)
        {
            <p class="lede">@Text.Banner</p>
        }

        @*
            EVERY OUTCOME OF PRESSING A BUTTON RENDERS INSIDE THIS DIV, and that is the whole reason it
            exists rather than the notices sitting loose in <main>.

            The action bar is deliberately unpinned on mobile (see app.css), so Submit sits at the
            bottom of an eight-section form - about eight screens below this - and both outcomes, the
            refusal list and the confirmation, draw up here. Nothing scrolled, so pressing Submit on a
            phone produced no visible response at all and the claimant pressed it again.

            tabindex="-1" makes it programmatically focusable and nothing else: it is not in the tab
            order, and Tab from the last field still goes where it went before. Focus is what moves the
            claimant here - see FocusAsync in OnAfterRenderAsync - because a focus move both scrolls
            the element into view and is announced by a screen reader, where a scroll alone is silent.
        *@
        <div class="result" tabindex="-1" @ref="_result">

        @if (_draftUnavailable)
        {
            @* Said out loud rather than quietly handing back a blank form, which would read as "your
               draft is gone" to somebody whose draft is in fact fine and on another device. *@
            <div class="banner" role="alert">
                <h4>This draft is not available in this browser</h4>
                <p>
                    Drafts are stored in the browser they were written in. This one was saved
                    elsewhere, already submitted, or deleted. The form below is a new one.
                </p>
                <p><a href="/drafts">See the drafts saved in this browser</a></p>
            </div>
        }

        @if (_errors.Count > 0)
        {
            @*
                EVERY ITEM IS A LINK, and where the server named the field it is a link to that field
                rather than to its section. On a phone this list is the difference between fixing eight
                things and hunting for eight things through an eight-section form.

                The href is written against the current path for the same reason SectionRail's is: a
                fragment-only "#s3" resolves against <base href="/"> and navigates to the landing page.
                A refusal about the request rather than about the form - the server unreachable, no
                session - carries no anchor and stays a plain item, because there is nowhere on this
                page that would help.
            *@
            <div class="banner" role="alert">
                <h4>This form cannot be submitted yet</h4>
                <ul>
                    @foreach (SubmissionProblem problem in _errors)
                    {
                        <li>
                            @if (problem.Anchor.Length > 0)
                            {
                                <a href="@($"{PagePath}#{problem.Target}")">@problem.Message</a>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @problem.Message
                            }
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>
            </div>
        }

        @if (_submitted)
        {
            <div class="notice" role="status">
                <span class="ico">OK</span>
                <div>
                    @*
                        The short reference, not the submission id. "Keep that reference" printed above
                        36 hex characters was asking a volunteer to write down a GUID and read it back
                        over the phone to the office; nobody does, and nobody can. The GUID is still
                        what the PDF link below is addressed by - it stays the key, this is the name.
                    *@
                    <b>Submitted for approval.</b> Your claim reference is
                    <span class="refcode">@_reference</span>. Quote it in any correspondence about this
                    claim. An independent approver completes section 7, and Finance completes
                    section 8.
                    <div class="help">
                        <a href="@PdfUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the printable form (PDF)</a>
                        &mdash; this link works for anyone you send it to, which is how a reviewer reads
                        the claim. The receipts are not inside it; section 3 and the evidence manifest
                        name which file belongs to which purchase.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        }
        else if (_submissionId is not null)
        {
            <div class="notice" role="status">
                <span class="ico">OK</span>
                <div>
                    <b>Draft saved.</b> Your form is saved automatically and can be reopened from
                    <a href="/drafts">your saved drafts</a>. It has not been submitted, and no approver
                    can see it until you submit it.
                    <div class="help">
                        Drafts are stored in this browser. Clearing browsing data or changing device
                        will remove them. A draft is deleted after 90 days without changes.
                    </div>
                    @*
                        The same endpoint as the submitted link below, and on a DRAFT it answers only to
                        the session that owns it - PdfEndpoints takes the owner or a submitted claim, so
                        this link is private until the form is submitted and shareable afterwards.

                        Here mostly so the printed form can be shown while the output format is still
                        parked with the accountant, which until now meant fetching a URL by hand.
                    *@
                    <div class="help">
                        <a href="@PdfUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                            Preview the printable form (PDF)
                        </a>
                        &mdash; shows the draft as currently completed, and can only be opened from
                        this browser.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        }

        </div>

        @*
            EVERY CONTROL ON THE FORM IS INSIDE THIS FIELDSET, and one attribute on it is what stops a
            submitted claim being edited.

            It used to be the case that a successful submission left 52 inputs enabled and both action
            bar buttons live. The server defends itself - ErrorConstants.AlreadySubmitted has been there
            all along - so a claimant's reward for pressing the still-live Submit button was an error
            message about a state the page should never have offered.

            A <fieldset disabled> rather than a disabled attribute threaded through fifty-odd fields:
            the browser disables every descendant control natively, so nothing can be missed and nothing
            has to be remembered when a field is added. Its own box is turned off - border, padding and
            the min-width:min-content that would otherwise stop the .grid children shrinking inside it.

            Links are deliberately unaffected: <a> is not a form control, so the PDF stays reachable on
            a locked page, which is the whole point of the page after submission.
        *@
        <fieldset disabled="@_submitted"
                  style="border:0;padding:0;margin:0;min-width:0;display:block">

        <!-- 0 -->
        @*
            The question that decides the form. It is asked in the claimant's terms - whose money left
            whose account - rather than by naming the two documents, because somebody who has just bought
            morning tea knows which card they used and does not know what a "debit card purchase form" is.
        *@
        <section class="card" id="s0">
            <header>
                <span class="num">00</span>
                <h3>How was this paid for?</h3>
                <span class="hint">Determines which form applies</span>
            </header>
            <div class="body">
                @*
                    checked follows _pendingKind while a change is waiting to be confirmed, so the chip
                    the claimant just pressed stays lit while they read what it will cost - and goes back
                    on its own if they cancel.
                *@
                <div class="chips">
                    <label class="chip">
                        <input type="radio" name="kind"
                               checked="@((_pendingKind ?? Model.Kind) == SubmissionKind.ExpenseReimbursement)"
                               @onchange="() => ChooseKind(SubmissionKind.ExpenseReimbursement)"/>
                        Paid personally
                    </label>
                    <label class="chip">
                        <input type="radio" name="kind"
                               checked="@((_pendingKind ?? Model.Kind) == SubmissionKind.DebitCardPurchase)"
                               @onchange="() => ChooseKind(SubmissionKind.DebitCardPurchase)"/>
                        Paid by Church debit card
                    </label>
                </div>

                @if (_pendingKind is { } pending)
                {
                    @* Asked here rather than through a browser confirm(), which cannot say what is
                       about to be lost and reads as an error. *@
                    <div class="banner" role="alert" style="margin-top:14px">
                        <h4>Change this to @KindNoun(pending)?</h4>
                        <p>
                            The two forms ask different questions and carry different declarations, so
                            the answers you have already given cannot come across. Changing this clears
                            <b>section 4's six questions</b>, <b>section 6's five declarations</b>, and
                            the details that only belong to the form you are leaving.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Your name, ministry, section 2, every line in section 3 and every receipt you
                            have attached all stay exactly as they are.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <button class="b" type="button" @onclick="ConfirmKindChange">
                                Yes, change it and clear those answers
                            </button>
                            <button class="b" type="button" @onclick="CancelKindChange">Keep this form</button>
                        </p>
                    </div>
                }
                else if (Model.Kind is null)
                {
                    @* The chips are two words each now, so this line carries the whole distinction -
                       whose money left whose account. It is the sentence somebody who has just bought
                       morning tea reads to place themselves, and it stays in the second person. *@
                    <p class="help">
                        <b>Paid personally</b> &mdash; cash, your own card or your own account, and you
                        are claiming the money back. <b>Paid by Church debit card</b> &mdash; the money
                        has already left the Church's account and this form records what it bought.
                    </p>
                }
            </div>
        </section>

        @if (Model.Kind is not null)
        {
            <!-- 1 -->
            <section class="card" id="s1">
                <header>
                    <span class="num">01</span>
                    <h3>@Text.Section1Caption</h3>
                    <span class="hint">@Text.Section1Hint</span>
                </header>
                <div class="body">
                    @if (_prefilled)
                    {
                        <div class="notice" style="margin-bottom:16px">
                            <span class="ico">i</span>
                            <div>
                                Filled in from the last claim submitted in this browser. Check every
                                answer below and correct anything that has changed.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    <div class="grid">
                        <div class="f c5">
                            <label for="submitter">@Text.SubmitterNameLabel <span class="req">*</span></label>
                            @* autocomplete is the difference between one tap and typing a name out on a
                               phone keyboard. Only on the fields that are genuinely about the person -
                               ministry, supplier and the like are not autofillable and must not claim to
                               be. Nothing on this form carries a card autocomplete token: the only card
                               field here is the last four digits, and cc-number would invite the browser
                               to fill the full number into a form that must never hold one. *@
                            @* aria-invalid is set from the last refusal, never from the browser's own
                               :invalid - which fires on every empty required box while somebody is
                               still typing and would paint the form red before they finished reading
                               it. app.css draws the bad border off this attribute. *@
                            <input type="text" id="submitter" autocomplete="name"
                                   aria-invalid="@Invalid("submitter")"
                                   @bind="Model.SubmitterName" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>
                        <div class="f c3">
                            <label for="formdate">@Text.FormDateLabel <span class="req">*</span></label>
                            <input type="date" id="formdate" @bind="Model.FormDate" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>

                        @if (Model.Kind == SubmissionKind.DebitCardPurchase)
                        {
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="card4">Card last 4 digits <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                <input type="text" id="card4" class="mono" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="4"
                                       placeholder="0000" aria-invalid="@Invalid("card4")"
                                       @bind="Model.CardLastFourDigits" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                                <span class="help">Never record the full number, PIN or CVV.</span>
                            </div>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="contact">Phone / email <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                @* One field taking either, so it stays type=text: type=email would reject
                                   a phone number and type=tel would put the wrong keyboard up for an
                                   address. autocomplete="tel" is the more useful of the two on a phone. *@
                                <input type="text" id="contact" autocomplete="tel"
                                       @bind="Model.ContactPhoneEmail" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>
                        }

                        <div class="f c12">
                            <fieldset style="border:0;padding:0;margin:0">
                                <legend class="lbl" style="padding:0">Role / relationship <span class="req">*</span></legend>
                                <div class="chips">
                                    @foreach (ClaimantRole role in Enum.GetValues<ClaimantRole>())
                                    {
                                        <label class="chip">
                                            <input type="radio" name="role" checked="@(Model.Role == role)"
                                                   @onchange="() => SetRole(role)"/>
                                            @RoleLabel(role)
                                        </label>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            </fieldset>
                        </div>

                        @if (Model.Role == ClaimantRole.Other)
                        {
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="roleother">Other &mdash; please say what</label>
                                <input type="text" id="roleother" @bind="Model.RoleOther" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>
                        }

                        <div class="f c4">
                            <label for="ministry">Ministry / department <span class="req">*</span></label>
                            <input type="text" id="ministry" @bind="Model.MinistryDepartment" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>

                        @if (Model.Kind == SubmissionKind.DebitCardPurchase)
                        {
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="txdate">Transaction date <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                <input type="date" id="txdate" @bind="Model.TransactionDate" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="txtime">Time</label>
                                <input type="time" id="txtime" @bind="Model.TransactionTime" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>

                            <div class="f c5">
                                <label for="supplier">Supplier / merchant <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                <input type="text" id="supplier" @bind="Model.SupplierMerchant" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>
                            <div class="f c3">
                                <label for="charged">Amount charged <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                <div class="money">
                                    @* inputmode=decimal, not the browser's own type=number keyboard: iOS
                                       puts up numbers-and-punctuation for the latter, and a numeric pad
                                       for this. *@
                                    <input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="0.01" min="0" id="charged"
                                           aria-invalid="@Invalid("charged")"
                                           @bind="Model.AmountCharged" @bind:event="oninput" @bind:after="Touched"/>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="bankref">Bank reference</label>
                                <input type="text" id="bankref" class="mono" placeholder="e.g. EFTPOS 27/08"
                                       @bind="Model.BankReference" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="periodfrom">Expense period &mdash; from <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                <input type="date" id="periodfrom" @bind="Model.ExpensePeriodFrom" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>
                            <div class="f c4">
                                <label for="periodto">Expense period &mdash; to <span class="req">*</span></label>
                                <input type="date" id="periodto" @bind="Model.ExpensePeriodTo" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                            </div>

                            <div class="f c4">
                                <span class="lbl">Payment method <span class="req">*</span></span>
                                <div class="chips">
                                    <label class="chip">
                                        <input type="radio" name="paymethod"
                                               checked="@(Model.PaymentMethod == PaymentMethod.Eft)"
                                               @onchange="() => SetPaymentMethod(PaymentMethod.Eft)"/>EFT
                                    </label>
                                    <label class="chip">
                                        <input type="radio" name="paymethod"
                                               checked="@(Model.PaymentMethod == PaymentMethod.Other)"
                                               @onchange="() => SetPaymentMethod(PaymentMethod.Other)"/>Other
                                    </label>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            @if (Model.PaymentMethod == PaymentMethod.Other)
                            {
                                <div class="f c4">
                                    <label for="paymethodother">Other &mdash; please say what</label>
                                    <input type="text" id="paymethodother"
                                           @bind="Model.PaymentMethodOther" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                                </div>
                            }

                            <div class="f c4">
                                <span class="lbl">Bank details on file <span class="req">*</span></span>
                                <div class="yn">
                                    <label>
                                        <input type="radio" name="bankonfile" checked="@(Model.BankDetailsOnFile == false)"
                                               @onchange="() => SetBankDetails(false)"/>No &ndash; provide securely
                                    </label>
                                    <label>
                                        <input type="radio" name="bankonfile" checked="@(Model.BankDetailsOnFile == true)"
                                               @onchange="() => SetBankDetails(true)"/>Yes
                                    </label>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>

                    @*
                        Reproduced from the paper forms, and a different warning on each. The
                        reimbursement one is also why this page asks for no BSB and no account number:
                        the form deliberately collects neither, and neither does this app.
                    *@
                    <div class="notice" style="margin-top:16px">
                        <span class="ico">!</span>
                        <div>
                            <b>@Text.Section1NoticeTitle</b> @Text.Section1NoticeBody
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 2 -->
            <section class="card" id="s2">
                <header>
                    <span class="num">02</span>
                    <h3>@Text.Section2Caption</h3>
                </header>
                <div class="body">
                    <div class="grid">
                        @*
                            One field, one column, a per-kind label - "Ministry / department" on the card
                            form and "Purpose / activity" on the reimbursement one. It reads oddly beside
                            section 1's field of the same name on a card form, but that is what the paper
                            prints, and paper-form-fields.md is the authority.

                            Until the two pages were merged the card form had no such field at all, so
                            PurposeActivity was null on every card submission - a blank row in the PDF and
                            a missing subtitle in the drafts list.
                        *@
                        <div class="f c6">
                            <label for="activity">@Text.PurposeActivityLabel <span class="req">*</span></label>
                            <input type="text" id="activity" @bind="Model.PurposeActivity" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>
                        <div class="f c6">
                            <label for="event">Event / project</label>
                            <input type="text" id="event" @bind="Model.EventProject" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>
                        <div class="f c4">
                            <label for="approvedby">Prior approval by</label>
                            <input type="text" id="approvedby" @bind="Model.PriorApprovalBy" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>
                        <div class="f c4">
                            <label for="approvaldate">Approval date</label>
                            <input type="date" id="approvaldate" @bind="Model.ApprovalDate" @bind:event="oninput"/>
                        </div>
                        <div class="f c12">
                            <label for="purpose">@Text.PurposeNarrativePrompt <span class="req">*</span></label>
                            <textarea id="purpose" rows="4" aria-invalid="@Invalid("purpose")"
                                      @bind="Model.PurposeNarrative" @bind:event="oninput"></textarea>
                            @if (Text.PurposeNarrativeHint is { } hint)
                            {
                                <span class="help">@hint</span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            @*
                Section 3 and the attachments used to be two cards, in that order, and a claimant had to
                keep the rows in one lined up with the files in the other by eye. They are one card now:
                a purchase is created BY attaching its receipt, and its questions are asked underneath
                that receipt. See ExpenseDetailsSection.
            *@
            @* Invalid travels down so the fields a refusal named can be marked wherever they live. The
               ids are the ones each component builds; the server names them by the same rule. *@
            <ExpenseDetailsSection Model="Model" Text="Text" Files="_attachments" Invalid="InvalidFields"
                                   EnsureSubmission="EnsureSubmissionAsync" OnChanged="Touched"/>

            <ComplianceSection Model="Model" Text="Text" OnChanged="Touched"/>

            <DeclarationsSection Model="Model" Text="Text" Attachments="_attachments"
                                 Invalid="InvalidFields" OnChanged="Touched"/>

            <ApprovalSections Kind="Model.Kind.Value"/>

            <p class="footnote">@Text.Footnote</p>
        }

        </fieldset>

        @*
            What stands where the action bar used to be. Somebody who has just submitted scrolls to the
            bottom of the form looking for the button they pressed; finding nothing there at all reads
            as the page having broken rather than as the claim having been filed.
        *@
        @if (_submitted)
        {
            <p class="submitted-note">
                This claim has been submitted and can no longer be edited. Your reference is
                <span class="refcode">@_reference</span>.
                <a href="@PdfUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the printable form (PDF)</a>.
                To correct anything on it, contact Finance and quote the reference.
            </p>
        }
    </main>
</div>

@*
    Hidden, not disabled, until question zero is answered. A greyed-out Submit still reads as a control
    somebody is expected to reach; an absent one says the form has not started yet, which is the truth.
    There is also nothing for these buttons to act on - every one of them needs a kind.

    Gone entirely once the claim is submitted, for the same reason rather than a different one. The
    fieldset above would grey these out, but a greyed Save draft and a greyed Submit for approval on a
    filed claim invite somebody to work out how to re-enable them. There is nothing left to save and
    nothing left to submit; the submitted note above says so in words.

    If amendment after submission ever becomes a real requirement it needs a deliberate control of its
    own - "Request a change" - and not the Submit button being live again.
*@
@if (Model.Kind is not null && !_submitted)
{
<div class="actionbar">
    <div class="sum">
        @if (Model.Kind == SubmissionKind.DebitCardPurchase)
        {
            @* The card form's total is stated twice - once by the bank, once by the claimant's
               itemisation - and the two have to agree. Nothing external states the reimbursement
               form's total, so it shows its own subtotal instead. *@
            <div><span class="k">Card charge</span><span class="v">@(Model.AmountCharged?.ToString("C2") ?? "$0.00")</span></div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div><span class="k">@Text.GrossTotalLabel</span><span class="v">@Model.GrossTotal.ToString("C2")</span></div>
        }
        <div><span class="k">@Text.NetTotalLabel</span><span class="v">@Model.NetTotal.ToString("C2")</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">Purchases</span><span class="v">@Model.Details.Count</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">Files</span><span class="v">@_attachments.Count</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="spacer"></div>
    @if (Model.ChargeMismatch)
    {
        <span class="badge warn">Receipts do not match the card charge</span>
    }
@*
    Development builds only.

    Gated on a `const bool`, NOT on a #if around the markup: Razor does not process C# preprocessor
    directives in the markup section at all. Writing `#if DEBUG` there renders the words "#if DEBUG"
    into the page and the button ships anyway - observed on 2026-08-31, the action bar read
    "#if DEBUG / Fill with mock data / #endif".

    A const false is genuinely compiled out: Roslyn removes the unreachable branch, and MockData itself
    plus FillWithMockData are inside #if DEBUG in the C# sections, where directives do work. So in a
    published build neither the control nor the generator exists - which is the requirement, because a
    button that ships and is merely hidden by CSS is still a button, and this one writes rows.
*@
    @if (MockDataAvailable)
    {
        <button class="b" type="button" @onclick="FillWithMockData" disabled="@_busy">Fill with mock data</button>
    }
    <button class="b" type="button" @onclick="Save" disabled="@_busy">
        @(_busy ? "Saving…" : "Save draft")
    </button>
    <button class="b primary" type="button" @onclick="SubmitForApproval" disabled="@_busy">
        Submit for approval
    </button>
</div>
}

@code
{
    [CascadingParameter] private MainLayout Layout { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>What the page is called before question zero has been answered.</summary>
    private const string UnchosenTitle = "Expense form";

    private ExpenseFormModel Model { get; set; } = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// Every word on the page below section 0, in the chosen form's own wording.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Throws rather than falling back to a kind while none is chosen. There is no safe default: the
    /// two documents disagree on four of six compliance questions and four of five declarations, so a
    /// fallback would print one form's compliance text to somebody filling in the other. Every use is
    /// inside the <c>@if (Model.Kind is not null)</c> that wraps sections 1 to 8.
    /// </remarks>
    private ExpenseFormText Text => ExpenseFormWording.For(
        Model.Kind ?? throw new InvalidOperationException(
            "The form's wording was read before question zero was answered."));

    /// <summary>
    /// Everything wrong with the form, as the banner renders it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <see cref="SubmissionProblem"/> rather than strings, because a refusal has to know where it
    /// sends the claimant. Problems raised on this page - a server that cannot be reached, question
    /// zero unanswered before an upload - carry an empty anchor and render as plain text: there is no
    /// field on the form that would help.
    /// </remarks>
    private readonly List<SubmissionProblem> _errors = [];

    private readonly List<ExpenseAttachment> _attachments = [];
    private bool _busy;
    private bool _submitted;

    /// <summary>
    /// The claim reference the server issued at submission - <c>RE-2026-0142</c>. Null until the form
    /// is submitted, because a draft is not a claim and has no reference.
    /// </summary>
    private string? _reference;

    /// <summary>
    /// The block every outcome renders into, at the top of the form. Focused after a Save or a Submit
    /// so the claimant is taken to the answer.
    /// </summary>
    private ElementReference _result;

    /// <summary>
    /// Set when a button press has produced something to read, and cleared by the render that acts on
    /// it. A flag rather than an await inside the handler, because the notice does not exist in the DOM
    /// until the render that follows the handler - focusing before that focuses the previous state.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _showResult;

    /// <summary>
    /// The draft to resume, from the route. Null on the plain <c>/forms/expense</c> route, which starts
    /// a new one.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Guid? DraftId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The draft's id once it exists. Phase one of the two-phase write: receipts are uploaded against
    /// this, and only then can the form be submitted.
    /// </summary>
    private Guid? _submissionId;

    /// <summary>
    /// The printable form for this submission. Only rendered where <see cref="_submissionId"/> is set,
    /// which is what the null-forgiving read below relies on.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A plain link rather than a fetch: the endpoint is same-origin behind YARP, so the draft session
    /// cookie rides along and the browser's own PDF viewer opens it.
    /// </remarks>
    private string PdfUrl => $"/api/submissions/{_submissionId}/pdf";

    /// <summary>
    /// This page's own path, for the refusal banner's links.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The same trap <c>SectionRail.Href</c> documents: a fragment-only href resolves against
    /// <c>&lt;base href="/"&gt;</c>, so "#s3" becomes "https://host/#s3" and every link in the banner
    /// navigates to the landing page instead of moving down the form. Nothing errors, so it reads as
    /// "the links just do not work".
    /// </remarks>
    private string PagePath => new Uri(Navigation.Uri).GetLeftPart(UriPartial.Path);

    /// <summary>
    /// The ids of the fields the last refusal named, so each one can be marked <c>aria-invalid</c> and
    /// drawn with the bad border. Empty whenever the banner is.
    /// </summary>
    private HashSet<string> InvalidFields =>
        _errors.Where(x => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(x.FieldId)).Select(x => x.FieldId!).ToHashSet();

    /// <summary>
    /// <c>"true"</c> when this field is one the last refusal named, and null otherwise - null so Blazor
    /// omits the attribute entirely rather than rendering <c>aria-invalid="false"</c> on fifty fields.
    /// </summary>
    private string? Invalid(string fieldId) => InvalidFields.Contains(fieldId) ? "true" : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// A problem raised here rather than by the server. It has no section to send anybody to.
    /// </summary>
    private void AddError(string message) => _errors.Add(new SubmissionProblem(message, string.Empty));

    /// <summary>
    /// Set when the route named a draft this session does not own, or one that has been submitted. The
    /// page says so rather than silently handing back an empty form, which would look like the draft
    /// had been lost.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _draftUnavailable;

    /// <summary>
    /// Section 1 arrived filled in from the last claim on this browser. Said on screen rather than done
    /// quietly: a form that has answers in it that nobody remembers typing is a form somebody submits
    /// without reading, and the whole of section 1 is a statement about who is making the claim.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _prefilled;

    private DraftAutosave? _autosave;

    /// <summary>
    /// A re-answer of question zero that is waiting to be confirmed, because taking it would clear
    /// answers the claimant has already given. Null the rest of the time, including for the first
    /// answer and for a change that costs nothing.
    /// </summary>
    private SubmissionKind? _pendingKind;

    private static string KindNoun(SubmissionKind kind) => kind == SubmissionKind.DebitCardPurchase
        ? "a Church debit card purchase"
        : "an expense you paid personally";

    private IReadOnlyList<SectionRail.RailSection> Rail
    {
        get
        {
            List<SectionRail.RailSection> rail =
                [new("0", "How this was paid for", "s0", Complete: Model.Kind is not null)];

            // The rest of the form does not exist until the question is answered, and a rail entry for
            // a section that is not on screen is a dead link.
            if (Model.Kind is null)
                return rail;

            rail.AddRange(
            [
                new("1", Text.Section1Caption, "s1", Complete: !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.SubmitterName)),
                new("2", Text.Section2Caption, "s2", Complete: !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.PurposeNarrative)),
                // One rail entry, where there were two. Section 3 and "Attachments" were separate cards
                // until the receipts became what section 3 is made of; a rail link to a card that no
                // longer exists is a dead link.
                new("3", Text.Section3Caption, "s3",
                    Complete: Model.Details.Count > 0
                              && Model.Details.All(x => x.Answered && x.TotalIncGst > 0)),
                new("4", Text.Section4Caption, "s4", Complete: Model.Compliance.All(x => x is not null))
            ]);

            // Only listed when some purchase has nothing from the shop behind it - the paper form's own
            // rule is "complete only when evidence is unavailable".
            if (Model.HasMissingEvidence(_attachments))
                rail.Add(new SectionRail.RailSection("5", "Missing receipt", "s5", Model.MissingDeclared));

            rail.AddRange(
            [
                new("6", Text.Section6Caption, "s6", Complete: Model.Declarations.All(x => x)),
                // Pending forever, as far as this scope is concerned: an approver and finance fill these
                // in, and neither is on this page.
                new("7", "Independent approval", "s7", Complete: false, Pending: true),
                new("8", "Finance use only", "s8", Complete: false, Pending: true)
            ]);

            return rail;
        }
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        Layout.SetTitle(UnchosenTitle);

        _autosave = new DraftAutosave(SaveQuietlyAsync);

        if (DraftId is not { } id)
        {
            // A NEW form. Section 1 is filled in from the last claim filed on this browser, which takes
            // a routine claim from about fifteen fields to about six. Only on a new one: a resumed draft
            // already carries its own answers, and overwriting them with older ones would be the form
            // undoing somebody's typing.
            //
            // Nothing is written to the server by this. The autosave only runs once something is
            // touched, so a pre-filled form that is then abandoned still leaves no row behind - which
            // is the whole reason the draft is created lazily.
            if (await Claimant.ReadAsync() is { } remembered)
            {
                Model.SubmitterName = remembered.Name;
                Model.ContactPhoneEmail = remembered.ContactPhoneEmail;
                Model.Role = remembered.Role;
                Model.RoleOther = remembered.RoleOther;
                Model.MinistryDepartment = remembered.Ministry;

                _prefilled = true;
            }

            return;
        }

        // Resuming. The server decides whether this session may have it - see IExpenseSubmissionService
        // - so nothing here needs to check, and a refusal is indistinguishable from a draft that does
        // not exist, which is deliberate.
        try
        {
            BasicReadResponse<ExpenseSubmission> response =
                await Submissions.Read(new ReadExpenseSubmissionRequest { SubmissionId = id });

            if (response is { Success: true, Entity: not null })
            {
                // The kind comes back with the draft, so a resumed form arrives with question zero
                // already answered - and answered by the stored row rather than by the route, which is
                // what decides what every per-kind field in it means.
                Model = ExpenseFormModel.FromSubmission(response.Entity);
                _submissionId = response.Entity.Id;

                Layout.SetTitle(Text.PageTitle);

                // The receipts already attached. Without these the attachments card renders empty on a
                // form that has three files against it, and attaching them again is the obvious next
                // move - the one that would look like it worked.
                _attachments.AddRange(response.Entity.Attachments);
            }
            else
            {
                _draftUnavailable = true;
            }
        }
        // A REFUSAL IS NOT A SERVER FAULT. Read is behind the AnonymousSession policy, so a browser with
        // no session - a link to a draft opened in a different browser, or after the cookie expired - is
        // refused before the method runs. That has to land as "this draft is unavailable", the same
        // answer as a draft that does not exist, rather than as "could not reach the server".
        catch (RpcException rpc) when (DraftSession.IsRefusal(rpc))
        {
            _draftUnavailable = true;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _draftUnavailable = true;
            AddError($"Could not reach the server: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes the claimant to whatever the last button press produced.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>FocusAsync</c> rather than a <c>scrollIntoView</c> through a JS file of our own, and it is
    /// the better tool rather than the cheaper one: moving focus scrolls the element into view <b>and</b>
    /// is announced, where a scroll on its own leaves somebody using a screen reader exactly where they
    /// were with no idea anything happened. It also keeps this app's only JavaScript the theme script
    /// in index.html, which has a reason to be a script that this does not.
    /// <para>
    /// The autosave never sets the flag. This fires when somebody has pressed a button and is waiting
    /// for an answer; a page that jumped to the top on its own two seconds after every keystroke would
    /// be unusable.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!_showResult)
            return;

        _showResult = false;

        await _result.FocusAsync();
    }

    public void Dispose() => _autosave?.Dispose();

#if DEBUG
    private const bool MockDataAvailable = true;
#else
    private const bool MockDataAvailable = false;
#endif

    /// <summary>
    /// Answers question zero, which is what brings the rest of the form on screen - or, when the answer
    /// is being changed and that would cost something, asks first.
    /// </summary>
    private Task ChooseKind(SubmissionKind kind)
    {
        if (Model.Kind == kind)
        {
            _pendingKind = null;

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        if (Model.KindChangeWouldClear(kind))
        {
            // Nothing is changed yet. The model still holds the old kind and every answer under it
            // until ConfirmKindChange, so cancelling is genuinely free rather than an undo.
            _pendingKind = kind;

            StateHasChanged();

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        return ApplyKind(kind);
    }

    private Task ConfirmKindChange() =>
        _pendingKind is { } kind ? ApplyKind(kind) : Task.CompletedTask;

    private void CancelKindChange()
    {
        _pendingKind = null;

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private Task ApplyKind(SubmissionKind kind)
    {
        _pendingKind = null;

        Model.SwitchKind(kind);

        Layout.SetTitle(Text.PageTitle);

        // Bumping the autosave ONLY for a change to an existing draft. On the very first answer there is
        // nothing to save and nowhere to save it, and writing then would leave a row behind for somebody
        // who answered one question and walked away - the litter the lazy create exists to avoid,
        // observed on 2026-09-01 as an "Unnamed draft" on the drafts page. Once a row exists the
        // opposite is true: the stored kind decides what its columns mean, and it must not be stale.
        if (_submissionId is not null)
            return Touched();

        StateHasChanged();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Replaces the form with a randomly chosen plausible scenario, receipts and all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Two presses give visibly different submissions, every press exercises all three itemisation
    /// modes, and roughly one in three also takes the awkward paths - a purchase evidenced only by a bank
    /// line, which opens section 5, and a Yes that opens section 4's reveal.
    /// <para>
    /// <b>This uploads files, which it did not have to before, and the result is not submittable without
    /// them.</b> Section 3 is built out of attachments now: a purchase is created by attaching a receipt,
    /// and <c>Submit</c> refuses one with nothing against it. So the generator fills the form, this saves
    /// it to get an id, and then a generated PNG goes up against each purchase - see
    /// <see cref="MockReceipt"/> for why they are real PNGs and why each one differs.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task FillWithMockData()
    {
#if DEBUG
        _busy = true;

        try
        {
            // A fresh model rather than mutating the current one: leftover rows from a previous press
            // would make two mock submissions differ in ways the generator did not choose, which defeats
            // the point. The kind is carried over, because it is the claimant's answer and not the
            // generator's to pick.
            Model = new ExpenseFormModel { Kind = Model.Kind };

            MockData.Fill(Model);

            // A new draft, not the one already created - otherwise the second press would silently add
            // receipts and purchases to the first submission.
            _submissionId = null;
            _attachments.Clear();
            _errors.Clear();

            // Saved before the uploads, because an upload needs an id to go against. Everything below is
            // skipped if that fails, leaving a filled-in form with no evidence - which is a state the
            // page shows honestly rather than one it pretends away.
            if (await EnsureSubmissionAsync() is not { } submissionId)
                return;

            // A bank line instead of a receipt on the last purchase, when the generator opened section 5.
            // This is what actually triggers it: the declaration is required because of what is attached,
            // not because a field on the form says so.
            bool lastIsBankLine = Model.MissingDeclared;

            for (int index = 0; index < Model.Details.Count; index++)
            {
                ExpenseDetailModel detail = Model.Details[index];

                bool last = index == Model.Details.Count - 1;

                AttachmentKind kind = lastIsBankLine && last
                    ? AttachmentKind.BankOrCardStatement
                    : AttachmentKind.SupplierReceipt;

                byte[] png = MockReceipt.Png(index + 1);

                using MemoryStream content = new(png);

                AttachmentUploadResult result = await Attachments.UploadAsync(
                    submissionId, detail.Key, MockReceipt.FileName(index + 1), "image/png", content, kind);

                if (result is { Success: true, Attachment: not null })
                    _attachments.Add(result.Attachment);
                else
                    AddError($"Mock receipt {index + 1}: {result.Error}");
            }

            // A second save, so the stored submission sees the section 5 declaration that the uploads
            // have just made applicable. Update evaluates that trigger against the attachments, and on
            // the first save there were none.
            await EnsureSubmissionAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
        }

        await Touched();
#else
        // Unreachable: MockDataAvailable is a const false in Release, so the only call site is compiled
        // away. The method itself has to stay declared, because Razor resolves @onclick against it while
        // building the render tree even for a branch it then eliminates.
        await Task.CompletedTask;
#endif
    }

    private void SetRole(ClaimantRole role)
    {
        Model.Role = role;

        // Dropping the free text when the role moves away from Other, so a stale "Other" answer cannot
        // ride along on a submission that no longer says Other.
        if (role != ClaimantRole.Other)
            Model.RoleOther = null;
    }

    private void SetPaymentMethod(PaymentMethod method)
    {
        Model.PaymentMethod = method;

        // Same rule as SetRole: a stale answer must not ride along on a submission that no longer
        // says Other.
        if (method != PaymentMethod.Other)
            Model.PaymentMethodOther = null;
    }

    private void SetBankDetails(bool onFile) => Model.BankDetailsOnFile = onFile;

    private static string RoleLabel(ClaimantRole role) => role switch
    {
        ClaimantRole.ResponsiblePerson => "Responsible Person",
        _ => role.ToString()
    };

    /// <summary>Re-renders for the running totals, and starts the autosave clock.</summary>
    private Task Touched()
    {
        StateHasChanged();
        _autosave?.Bump();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The autosave's save step. Deliberately silent about refusals.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>The autosave never puts a refusal on screen.</b> A draft no longer has to be a finished form -
    /// the completeness rules moved to submit on 2026-09-01 - so what reaches here is a genuine problem
    /// with what was typed, like an amount that does not parse or a card field with a letter in it. Even
    /// those are dropped: a form that scolds mid-keystroke is worse than one that waits. The claimant
    /// sees the problem when they press Save or Submit, which is when they have asked.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task SaveQuietlyAsync()
    {
        if (_submitted)
            return;

        await EnsureSubmissionAsync();

        _errors.Clear();

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns the draft's id, creating it on first use.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Called by the attachments card before its first upload, and by Save. Creating the draft lazily -
    /// rather than on page load - means somebody who opens the form and walks away leaves no row behind,
    /// which matters when nothing is authenticated and nothing cleans up. It is also what lets question
    /// zero stay changeable for as long as nothing has been written.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task<Guid?> EnsureSubmissionAsync()
    {
        // Cleared on EVERY attempt. Without this the banner accumulated the previous attempt's errors
        // alongside the new ones, so a problem the claimant had just fixed stayed on screen - observed
        // on 2026-08-31, where correcting the amount charged left the old mismatch listed twice.
        _errors.Clear();

        // Nothing to write yet, and nothing that could be written: the kind decides what every stored
        // field means, so a row cannot exist before question zero is answered. Reachable from the
        // attachments card, whose upload button does not know about section 0.
        if (Model.Kind is null)
        {
            _errors.Add(new SubmissionProblem(
                "Section 0: state how this was paid for. It determines which form applies.", "s0"));

            return null;
        }

        // The draft is created as soon as the first receipt is attached, long before the claimant has
        // finished typing. Everything since then has to reach the server, or submit checks a row that no
        // longer matches the screen.
        if (_submissionId is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                BasicResponse update = await Submissions.Update(new UpdateExpenseSubmissionRequest
                {
                    SubmissionId = _submissionId.Value,
                    Form = Model.ToCreateRequest(_attachments)
                });

                if (update.Success)
                    return _submissionId;

                foreach (string error in update.Errors.Count > 0
                             ? update.Errors
                             : [update.Error ?? "The draft could not be saved."])
                {
                    AddError(error);
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                AddError($"Could not reach the server: {ex.Message}");
            }

            return null;
        }

        try
        {
            BasicReadResponse<Guid?> response = await Submissions.Create(Model.ToCreateRequest(_attachments));

            if (response is { Success: true, Entity: not null })
            {
                _submissionId = response.Entity;

                return _submissionId;
            }

            // Errors is the list; Error is only its first entry. Show all of them - a form that reveals
            // its problems one at a time is the one people give up on.
            foreach (string error in response.Errors.Count > 0
                         ? response.Errors
                         : [response.Error ?? "The submission was refused."])
            {
                AddError(error);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            AddError($"Could not reach the server: {ex.Message}");
        }

        return null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Phase two. Saves the draft if that has not happened, then asks the server to check it is complete.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The server checks the STORED submission, not what this page currently holds - which is why the
    /// draft has to be saved first. Every refusal comes back at once rather than one at a time.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task SubmitForApproval()
    {
        _busy = true;
        _submitted = false;

        try
        {
            // EnsureSubmissionAsync below pushes the current state, so the pending autosave has nothing
            // left to contribute - and would land a second later as an Update against a submission that
            // is by then Submitted, putting a refusal on screen underneath the success message.
            _autosave?.Cancel();

            // Pushes any edits made since the draft was created, and clears the previous attempt's
            // errors, before the server is asked to check what it now holds.
            Guid? id = await EnsureSubmissionAsync();

            if (id is null)
                return;

            SubmitExpenseSubmissionResponse response = await Submissions.Submit(
                new SubmitExpenseSubmissionRequest { SubmissionId = id.Value });

            if (response.Success)
            {
                // Nothing to clear locally any more. The draft became a Submitted row, which is what
                // takes it out of ListDrafts - the browser holds no copy of its own to go stale.
                _submitted = true;

                // The claim reference, minted by the server in the same call. There is no second way
                // to fetch it: Read refuses a submitted claim by design.
                _reference = response.Reference;

                // Section 1's answers, for the next claim filed on this browser. On submit rather than
                // on every autosave: a draft is a form somebody is still deciding about, and
                // remembering one would hand the next claim whatever was on screen when they gave up.
                await Claimant.RememberAsync(new ClaimantMemory.Claimant
                {
                    Name = Model.SubmitterName,
                    ContactPhoneEmail = Model.ContactPhoneEmail,
                    Role = Model.Role,
                    RoleOther = Model.RoleOther,
                    Ministry = Model.MinistryDepartment
                });
            }
            else if (response.Problems.Count > 0)
            {
                // The refusals as the server built them, each carrying its section and, where there is
                // one, its field - which is what makes the banner a list of links and marks the fields.
                _errors.AddRange(response.Problems);
            }
            else
            {
                // A refusal about the request rather than about the form: no session, no such
                // submission, already submitted. Nothing on this page to point at.
                AddError(response.Error ?? "The submission was refused.");
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            AddError($"Could not reach the server: {ex.Message}");
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;

            // Whichever way it went - the confirmation or the list of refusals - there is now
            // something at the top of the page that the claimant pressed a button to be told.
            _showResult = true;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The explicit save. The autosave has usually beaten it to the punch; this is what a claimant
    /// presses to be told whether the form is being kept, and the one place its refusals are shown.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Save()
    {
        _busy = true;

        try
        {
            _autosave?.Cancel();

            await EnsureSubmissionAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
            _showResult = true;
        }
    }
}
